    "excerpt": "A plain-English breakdown of GoHighLevel pricing in 2026: what Starter, Unlimited and SaaS Pro include, the real usage costs, and which plan actually fits you.",
    "cover_image": "",
    "reading_time": 8,
    "content": "<p>If you&rsquo;re weighing up GoHighLevel, the first question is always the same: how much does it actually cost? The headline plans are simple enough &mdash; three tiers &mdash; but the subscription is only part of the picture. There are usage charges, add-ons, and an AI layer that can quietly add up. This guide gives you the honest version of <strong>GoHighLevel pricing in 2026</strong> so you can pick the right plan the first time.</p><p>Quick caveat before we start: prices move. The numbers below are accurate at the time of writing, but always confirm the current figures on the official GoHighLevel site before you commit.</p><h2>The three main GoHighLevel plans</h2><p>GoHighLevel sells one platform at three price points. The features overlap heavily &mdash; what changes between tiers is how many sub-accounts you get and whether you can run it as your own software business.</p><h3>Starter &mdash; around $97/mo</h3><p>The entry plan gives you the full GoHighLevel toolkit: CRM, funnels and websites, email and SMS marketing, calendars, pipelines, automation, and reputation management. The catch is scale &mdash; Starter caps you at up to three sub-accounts and does not include white-label branding or rebilling.</p><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> a solo operator or small business running their own marketing, or an agency with one or two clients testing the waters.</li><li><strong>The ceiling:</strong> you hit the sub-account limit fast once you start onboarding clients.</li></ul><h3>Unlimited &mdash; around $297/mo</h3><p>This is the workhorse agency plan. The defining feature is in the name: <strong>unlimited sub-accounts</strong>. You can add as many client accounts as you like without your platform cost going up. You also unlock white-label branding and rebilling at cost &mdash; you can pass usage charges (SMS, email, AI) through to clients without applying a markup.</p><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> agencies actively managing multiple clients who need room to grow.</li><li><strong>The upgrade trigger:</strong> the moment you outgrow three sub-accounts, $297 almost always beats stacking Starter limits.</li></ul><h3>Agency Pro / SaaS Pro &mdash; around $497/mo</h3><p>This is where GoHighLevel becomes a business model, not just a tool. SaaS Pro unlocks <strong>SaaS Mode</strong>: you package GoHighLevel under your own brand, set your own subscription tiers, and resell it as software. It also adds the features that make that viable &mdash; <strong>rebilling with markup</strong> (so usage becomes a profit margin, not just a pass-through), advanced API access, and the white-label desktop and mobile apps.</p><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> agencies and founders who want recurring SaaS revenue under their own name.</li><li><strong>The upgrade trigger:</strong> you want SaaS Mode, automated client sign-up and billing, or the ability to mark up usage costs.</li></ul><h2>The real costs beyond the subscription</h2><p>This is the part most pricing pages gloss over. Your monthly plan covers the platform &mdash; it does not cover usage. Several services are billed separately from a prepaid wallet, and they apply on every plan.</p><ul><li><strong>SMS and phone:</strong> texts, calls, and phone numbers are pay-as-you-go on top of your subscription.</li><li><strong>Email:</strong> sends are metered, so high-volume email campaigns carry a usage cost.</li><li><strong>AI usage:</strong> Conversation AI, Voice AI, and content tools draw from your AI wallet per use.</li><li><strong>Rebilling math:</strong> on Unlimited you can rebill these at cost; on SaaS Pro you can add a markup and keep the difference.</li></ul><h3>Add-ons and the AI Employee</h3><p>GoHighLevel also sells the <strong>AI Employee</strong> &mdash; a bundle of Voice AI, Conversation AI, Reviews AI, and content tools. It&rsquo;s an add-on, not part of the base plan, and it&rsquo;s priced <em>per sub-account</em>: roughly $50/mo for the Growth tier or about $97/mo for Unlimited usage on each enabled location. That last detail matters &mdash; if you turn it on for ten clients, you&rsquo;re paying for ten, not one. Budget for it deliberately rather than switching it on everywhere by default.</p><h2>Trial and annual discounts</h2><p>There&rsquo;s a <strong>14-day free trial</strong> with full access, so you can build and test before paying. There is no permanently free plan &mdash; only the trial. If you already know you&rsquo;re committing, paying annually typically saves you roughly 17% versus monthly billing across all three tiers. Again, confirm the exact discount on the official site, as promotions change.</p><h2>Which GoHighLevel plan should you choose?</h2><p>Skip the feature-by-feature paralysis. In practice, the decision comes down to who you are.</p><ul><li><strong>Solo or small business:</strong> Starter at ~$97 is plenty. You get the full platform and you don&rsquo;t need sub-accounts you&rsquo;ll never use.</li><li><strong>Growing agency:</strong> Unlimited at ~$297 is the obvious home. Unlimited sub-accounts and white-label branding pay for themselves once you have a handful of clients.</li><li><strong>Reseller / SaaS founder:</strong> SaaS Pro at ~$497 is the only tier that makes sense. SaaS Mode, rebilling with markup, and advanced API turn the platform into a revenue engine.</li></ul><p>A simple rule: pick the cheapest plan that doesn&rsquo;t block your business model. Don&rsquo;t pay for SaaS Mode you won&rsquo;t use &mdash; but don&rsquo;t cripple your agency on Starter to save $200 either.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is there a free GoHighLevel plan?</h3><p>No. There&rsquo;s a 14-day free trial with full access, but no permanently free tier. After the trial you move onto one of the paid plans.</p><h3>What is SaaS Pro?</h3><p>SaaS Pro (the Agency Pro plan at ~$497/mo) unlocks SaaS Mode, letting you rebrand and resell GoHighLevel as your own software with your own pricing, automated sign-up and billing, rebilling with markup, and advanced API access.</p><h3>Can I upgrade later?</h3><p>Yes. You can start on Starter or Unlimited and move up when you hit a real ceiling &mdash; outgrowing sub-accounts, or wanting SaaS Mode and markup rebilling. There&rsquo;s no need to overbuy on day one.</p><p>Still not sure which tier fits &mdash; or want it set up properly from the start? We help agencies and businesses choose the right plan and build the system around it. Take a look at our <a href='/services/ghl-setup'>GHL setup service</a>, then <a href='/contact'>book a free consult</a> and we&rsquo;ll map the most cost-effective path for you.</p>",
    "seo_title": "GoHighLevel Pricing 2026: Starter, Unlimited & SaaS Pro",
    "seo_description": "GoHighLevel pricing 2026 explained: Starter ($97), Unlimited ($297) and SaaS Pro ($497), plus usage costs, AI Employee and which plan actually fits you.",
    "seo_keywords": "gohighlevel pricing 2026, gohighlevel cost, ghl starter unlimited saas pro, how much is gohighlevel, gohighlevel plans",
