    "author": "GHL Prime Team",
    "excerpt": "A plain-English guide to what Zapier & GHL automation is, when to use it versus native GHL workflows, and 10 specific no-code workflows you can build today.",
    "cover_image": "",
    "reading_time": 8,
    "content": "<p>If you run an agency or a growing business on GoHighLevel, you have probably hit a wall: GHL handles everything <em>inside</em> your CRM beautifully, but at some point you need it to talk to an app that lives outside of it. That is where Zapier comes in. In this guide we explain plainly what Zapier &amp; GHL automation is, when to reach for it instead of a native GHL workflow, and 10 specific workflows you can build with no code.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s define the pieces first, because the term &lsquo;automation&rsquo; gets thrown around loosely.</p><h2>What is Zapier &amp; GHL automation?</h2><p><strong>Zapier</strong> is a no-code integration platform that connects thousands of apps together. It works on a simple model: a <strong>trigger</strong> (something happens in one app) kicks off one or more <strong>actions</strong> (something happens in another app). A single automation is called a &lsquo;Zap&rsquo;. You build it by pointing and clicking &mdash; no developers required.</p><p><strong>GoHighLevel (GHL)</strong> is an all-in-one CRM and marketing platform built for agencies and service businesses. It handles contacts, pipelines, calendars, email, SMS, funnels, and its own native automation engine called Workflows.</p><p>So <strong>what is Zapier &amp; GHL automation</strong>? It is the practice of using Zapier to connect GoHighLevel to other apps using triggers and actions, with no code. In Zapier, GHL appears as <strong>LeadConnector</strong> &mdash; the official, white-labeled HighLevel app. You authenticate with your HighLevel login, choose a sub-account, and you can then use triggers like <em>New Contact</em>, <em>New Opportunity</em>, or <em>Pipeline Stage Changed</em>, and actions like <em>Add or Update Contact</em>, <em>Add to Workflow</em>, and <em>Add Task</em>. When the native LeadConnector trigger or action you need does not exist, Zapier&rsquo;s Webhooks tool fills the gap.</p><h2>When to use Zapier vs native GHL workflows</h2><p>This is the question that saves you money and headaches, so here is the rule of thumb: <strong>use native GHL workflows for automation that stays inside the platform, and use Zapier when you need to connect to an outside app that GHL does not natively support.</strong></p><h3>Reach for native GHL workflows when&hellip;</h3><p>The whole automation lives inside GoHighLevel &mdash; a new lead triggering an email and SMS sequence, moving an opportunity through a pipeline, booking a calendar slot, assigning a task, or sending an internal notification. GHL workflows are included at no extra cost, your reporting stays unified, and you avoid stitching data across dashboards. As a habit, always check whether GHL can do it natively first.</p><h3>Reach for Zapier when&hellip;</h3><p>The trigger or the destination lives <em>outside</em> GHL. GHL workflows cannot natively listen to or push data into apps Zapier covers, such as Stripe, Google Sheets, Slack, Calendly, QuickBooks, Airtable, or your own internal tools. When the event happens in one of those apps, or you need to send GHL data into one of them, Zapier catches it and relays it. Used this way, Zapier complements GHL rather than replacing it &mdash; GHL stays your core system, and Zapier is the bridge to everything else.</p><h2>10 Zapier + GHL workflows worth building</h2><p>Each of these is a single Zap. They are practical, commonly requested, and easy to extend once you understand the trigger-and-action pattern.</p><h3>1. New GHL lead to a Google Sheets row</h3><p>Trigger on a new LeadConnector contact, then add a row to a Google Sheet. This GHL Zapier workflow gives sales teams, bookkeepers, or clients a live spreadsheet view without handing out CRM access &mdash; perfect for reporting and quick exports.</p><h3>2. New lead to a Slack channel alert</h3><p>When a new contact enters GHL, post a message to a Slack channel so your team gets an instant heads-up. Great for high-value inquiries where speed-to-lead matters and nobody should be refreshing the CRM all day.</p><h3>3. Stripe payment to a GHL contact and tag</h3><p>When a Stripe payment succeeds, create or update the contact in GHL and apply a tag like <em>paid-customer</em>. Because the payment event happens outside GHL, this is a textbook case for Zapier. The tag can then start a native GHL workflow for onboarding.</p><h3>4. Calendly or Acuity booking to a GHL appointment and contact</h3><p>When someone books in Calendly or Acuity, push the contact and booking details into GHL. If your audience prefers an external scheduler, this keeps your CRM as the single source of truth without forcing a tool switch.</p><h3>5. Typeform or Jotform submission to GHL</h3><p>Capture a Typeform or Jotform submission and create or update the matching GHL contact, mapping each form field cleanly. Ideal for quizzes, applications, and intake forms hosted outside GHL that still need to feed your pipeline.</p><h3>6. QuickBooks or Xero invoice to GHL</h3><p>When a new invoice is created or paid in QuickBooks or Xero, update the contact in GHL &mdash; tag them, add a note, or trigger a follow-up. This keeps your finance system and your CRM in sync without manual data entry.</p><h3>7. Mailchimp subscriber sync</h3><p>When someone subscribes in Mailchimp, add or update them as a GHL contact (and vice versa). Useful during a migration to GHL, or when a client insists on keeping a legacy Mailchimp list alive while you consolidate.</p><h3>8. Airtable record sync</h3><p>When a record is created or updated in Airtable, mirror it into GHL as a contact or opportunity. Plenty of teams run operations in Airtable; this GHL integration via Zapier connects that workspace to your sales and marketing engine.</p><h3>9. New GHL opportunity to a project tool</h3><p>When an opportunity is created or its pipeline stage changes in GHL, create a card or task in Asana, Trello, or ClickUp. The moment a deal becomes work, your delivery team sees it &mdash; no copy-paste handoff between sales and fulfillment.</p><h3>10. Inbound webhook to an internal or custom app</h3><p>Use Zapier&rsquo;s Webhooks tool to catch an inbound webhook from an internal system or a custom-built app, then create or update records in GHL. This is the catch-all for anything bespoke and the doorway to genuinely custom GoHighLevel Zapier automation.</p><h2>A few things to keep in mind</h2><p>GHL&rsquo;s API has rate limits, so very high-volume syncs need thoughtful design to avoid throttling. Field mapping matters &mdash; mismatched fields are the most common reason a Zap silently does the wrong thing. And before you build any of these, ask the native question first: if GHL can do it inside the platform, do it there and save the Zapier seat for the jobs only it can handle.</p><h2>Build it once, build it right</h2><p>Done well, Zapier becomes the quiet connective tissue between GoHighLevel and the rest of your stack. Done poorly, it becomes a tangle of half-working Zaps nobody understands. If you would rather have it mapped, built, and documented properly the first time, that is exactly the kind of work we do.</p><p>Explore our <a href='/services/automation'>GHL automation and workflows service</a> to see how we connect GHL to your wider toolset, or <a href='/contact'>get in touch</a> and tell us which of these workflows you want first.</p>",
    "seo_title": "What Is Zapier & GHL Automation? + 10 Workflows",
